BindOS Structure Inspector
==========================

This repository is derived from sokrypton/py2Dmol at revision
78c2d489d0b5c5d19accd9eeeef878c2868f5271.
Source: https://github.com/sokrypton/py2Dmol

The original BEER-WARE notice is retained verbatim in LICENSE.  The BindOS
changes add a local-only immutable-mmCIF/inspection-manifest interface, typed
validation at the package boundary, semantic inspection layers, and hashed
HTML/JSON/SVG/PNG export.  Arbitrary PDB/AFDB fetching is not part of the new
public interface; the legacy py2Dmol module remains included for attribution
and renderer reuse.

Attribution in exported bundles
-------------------------------

Every rendered HTML bundle inlines the py2Dmol viewer (~1 MB of minified
JavaScript), and minification strips the BEER-WARE notice from the source.
Since inspector 1.13 the renderer therefore writes the notice back into each
export -- as an HTML comment at the top of the file and as a visible credit
line at the foot of the page -- naming Sergey Ovchinnikov, the upstream
repository and the pinned revision.  The bundle is the artefact that travels,
so a notice kept only in this repository would not reach the person holding
the file.

Runtime dependencies:

- NumPy: BSD-3-Clause.
- Biopython: Biopython License Agreement (permissive BSD-style).
- IPython: BSD-3-Clause.
- gemmi (optional; used by the Claude Science skill to convert PDB input to
  mmCIF): MPL-2.0.  Not required by the renderer itself.

Code written in this repository rather than vendored:

- src/core/svg.js is a minimal 2D-context-to-SVG recorder described in its
  own header as a canvas2svg port.  It exports `window.C2S` for compatibility
  with the canvas2svg API.  If any of it is in fact derived from Gliffy's
  canvas2svg (MIT) rather than written against its interface, that project is
  owed an MIT notice here; the header should be made unambiguous either way.
- src/io/gif.js is a GIF89a encoder written here, deliberately in place of a
  CDN library, so the notebook viewer loads nothing but py2Dmol's own
  resources.

The reusable package must be distributed with LICENSE and this NOTICE.  Note
that setup.py currently declares no `license_files` and the repository has no
MANIFEST.in or pyproject.toml, so a built wheel ships neither file -- this
NOTICE's own requirement is not yet met by the packaging.
